\meta{status}{provisional}
\meta{status-text}{provisional until M4; AT-UP-8 is mandatory in its exit criteria.  OQ-UP-1: choose the concrete derived comma/framing construction and prove its discrete comparison. OQ-UP-2: Prop-valued existence classes are allowed; chosen witnesses are passed explicitly. No data-valued chosen-limit instance is introduced.}
\meta{supersedes}{dec-up-0009}
\meta{superseded-by}{none}
\tag{decision}
\tag{area:up}
\tag{status:provisional}

\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{Use \code{VertCore(Cat)} and later \code{VertCore(Cat_∞)} for categorical mapping spaces and (∞,1)-universal properties. For ordinary categories these mapping spaces are cores of functor categories. Construct exponentials/functor objects and verify their core-valued universal property; use their full functor categories when 2-dimensional information is required. Products and homotopy pullbacks here are understood in the (∞,1)-sense. A lax comma object is a separate 2-dimensional construction in \code{Vert₂} or the equipment. Do not identify it with a strict or homotopy pullback by terminology.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{The ambient dimension determines the universal property being asserted.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rejected}
  \p{Calling \code{VertRaw(Cat)} mapping spaces functor cores.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Acceptance}
  \p{\ref{at-up-0008}: for ordinary C,D the chosen exponential is the functor category and \code{MapCat(E,Fun(C,D)) ≃ MapCat(E×C,D)}, naturally in E; \ref{at-fd-0004}'s walking-isomorphism test.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{provisional until \ref{ms-0004}; \ref{at-up-0008} is mandatory in its exit criteria.}
  \p{\ref{oq-up-0001}: choose the concrete derived comma/framing construction and prove its discrete comparison. \ref{oq-up-0002}: Prop-valued existence classes are allowed; chosen witnesses are passed explicitly. No data-valued chosen-limit instance is introduced.}
}
